<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristippus of Cyrene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 435 – c. 356 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristippus of Cyrene was a Greek philosopher and pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> who founded the Cyrenaic school. He taught that the pleasure of the present moment is the highest good, while urging self-control so as to master pleasure rather than be ruled by it. Anecdotes describe him as a worldly man who charged fees for teaching and spent time at the court of Dionysius of Syracuse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristippus-of-cyrene.jpg" alt="Aristippus of Cyrene" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gottlieb Friedrich Riegel (1724–1784) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristippus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
